this is a particle robot alone it can't  do much just pulse out and back but as a  group these little BOTS can move towards  the light carry items even squeeze  through gaps and they can do those  things without any plan or central  coordination the useful behavior just  emerges from the way the group interacts  this is called stochastic mechanics and  it's the basis of many biological  systems think aunt Connie's or groups of  cells all these little robots can do is  change the regularity of their pulses in  response to light intensity add in some  loosely coupling magnets around the edge  and together the robots can push and  pull themselves along albeit slowly very  slowly the researchers say there are  some really distinct advantages to this  approach in theory robots that work in  this way could move through a whole host  of environments even places they've  never been before since they don't need  to assess plan or strategize they can be  flexible and essentially just muddle  through adapting in real time also like  a cluster of cells clusters of particle  robots don't need to be organized in a  particular structure or shape to work  they can just sort of move as an  amorphous blob the researchers modeled  their robots to see what might happen if  there were more safe a hundred or a  thousand of 10,000 and they found their  abilities scaled up along with the  number of BOTS  what's more they even disabled one in  five of them and the group prevailed all  this adds up to a robust system  something like you might find in biology  researchers say that in the future you  could put particle robots in any  environment mess up their order even  break a few and the system would still  work break a walking robots leg and  isn't going anywhere fast  because the output of the system relies  on complex interactions not complex  individuals each individual  but could be made even more simple while  still achieving high level tasks as a  group after that no central powerful  computer to coordinate the whole affair  an assistant seems a shoo-in for  miniaturization imagine swarms of  particle robot surgeons inside your body  but there's still work to do the  algorithms these particular BOTS use  might be tricky to translate into much  simpler or smaller robots but it is a  step or rather a pulsing shuffle in the  right direction  [Music]
 

